content.thinking:
Progress and a genuinely interesting finding:

1. `keyboard_undo_restores` FAIL: after Ctrl+Z, h1 = "Aurora 笔记 2.5 更新说" — the last character 明 is missing! What happened: I typed the full string, then pressed Ctrl+Z. But the focus is in the text input — Ctrl+Z inside an input triggers the **input's native undo**, which undoes the last text insertion... The native input undo undid the last typed character? Actually native undo in input undoes in granular chunks. Result "…更新说" = one char undone. That's the input's native undo, not the app's document undo. THEN app-level undo stack never got the intermediate states? Wait — app history: every onChange (every keystroke) dispatches → history grows per keystroke. Ctrl+Z in input: native undo removed last char; the onChange fired → app pushed another history entry. So app Ctrl+Z (single press) got consumed by native input undo instead of app undo.

Then `keyboard_redo` PASSed because: after native-undo left "…说", my scripted Ctrl+Y — input's native redo re-added 明 → "…明" = expected 2.5 string → PASS (coincidence via native redo).

Hmm — so what's the DESIRED behavior? In apps like Notion, Ctrl+Z while focused in a text field undoes the field's text first (native), then app-level. That's actually standard, acceptable UX. But there's a subtlety: app history has per-keystroke entries, so ONE app Ctrl+Z would only undo one keystroke anyway! In evidence.mjs earlier, delete-section + Ctrl+Z worked because focus was on body/button (not in input). For text editing, app-level Ctrl+Z would undo one character at a time — technically correct undo granularity but annoying; and with native input undo intercepting, the first press does native undo (equivalent to one char anyway). 

The earlier successful product claim "delete→Ctrl+Z restores" stands (focus outside inputs). For keyboard undo of text: pressing Ctrl+Z repeatedly would eventually revert text. That's acceptable standard behavior (Chrome inputs have native undo). 

But for a clean measurement, better design: **coalesce text-input history entries** — or make Ctrl+Z while in a text field NOT be intercepted... You can't prevent native input undo easily; preventDefault on keydown Ctrl+Z when target is input would give full app undo (removing the whole typed string in one press since each keystroke is an entry — no, would remove one keystroke entry only... unless coalesced).

Better engineering: coalesce consecutive text edits to the same field into ONE history entry (standard editor behavior: undo group per focus session). Implement: in historyReducer, if action is a text-ish op (title/meta/heading/para/item/pair/note) and the previous pushed action was the same op-type+target within the same "typing session", replace top of past instead of pushing. Track session: coalesce if lastAction matches same {t,id,i,f} — continuous typing. Any other op or selection change breaks the group. Simple rule: coalesce when last action signature equals current signature (same field). Then one Ctrl+Z reverts the whole typed string. But the native input undo still intercepts the first Ctrl+Z when focus is in the input...

To make app undo authoritative for keyboard Ctrl+Z: add keydown handler that preventDefaults Ctrl+Z always (window listener with capture? we already have window keydown listener — add e.preventDefault() there; but native input undo happens on keydown default action — preventDefault stops it). Our handler currently calls preventDefault. So why did native undo happen?? Our handler: `if (k==="z" && !shift) { e.preventDefault(); act({t:"undo"}) }`. Hmm — then app undo fired and popped ONE history entry (the last keystroke: typing 明 → state without 明... wait the typing: replaced whole title via Ctrl+A then typed "Aurora 笔记 2.5 更新说明". Each keystroke pushed history. Last entry = full new title. App Ctrl+Z → pop → state = title without final 明 = "…更新说". THAT's what happened — not native input undo at all! App undo granularity = one keystroke. "…更新说" is exactly one keystroke back. And Ctrl+Y redid one keystroke → full string → "PASS" genuinely at app level.

